I lay still on the slope where I could see between the branches, and looked towards the wood. All that side was as quiet as a church; nobody seemed to be about there, but I knew that behind the trees near the house there were a number of people in the shed, looking out of windows at what was going on. But here it was quite still; there was no sound except the falling of the rain and the drip, drip, drip from the eaves of the shed into the vats under them; and then suddenly there was another noise—a far-away noise—the sound of galloping hoofs. The wind brought it clearly to me.

The horse came nearer and nearer; now it must be just coming into the grounds;
